<h2 id="intersection-section-pushforward" class="tex-section"><span data-tag="0AZC">43.6</span> Proper pushforward</h2>

<p>
Suppose that $f : X \to Y$ is a proper morphism of varieties. Let $Z \subset X$ be a $k$-dimensional closed subvariety. We define $f_*[Z]$ to be $0$ if $\dim (f(Z)) < k$ and $d \cdot [f(Z)]$ if $\dim (f(Z)) = k$ where 
</p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  d = [\mathbf{C}(Z) : \mathbf{C}(f(Z))] = \deg (Z/f(Z))  \]
</div>
<p>
 is the degree of the dominant morphism $Z \to f(Z)$, see Morphisms, Definition <a href="/tag/02NY" data-tag="02NY">29.52.8</a>. Let $\alpha = \sum n_ i [Z_ i]$ be a $k$-cycle on $X$. The <em>pushforward</em> of $\alpha $ is the sum $f_* \alpha = \sum n_ i f_*[Z_ i]$ where each $f_*[Z_ i]$ is defined as above. This defines a homomorphism 
</p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  f_* : Z_ k(X) \longrightarrow Z_ k(Y)  \]
</div>
<p>
 See Chow Homology, Section <a href="/tag/02R3" data-tag="02R3">42.12</a>. 
</p>
<p>
<article class="env-lemma" id="0AZD">
       
      
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0AZD">Lemma <span data-tag="0AZD">43.6.1</span>.</a> Suppose that $f : X \to Y$ is a proper morphism of varieties. Let $\mathcal{F}$ be a coherent sheaf with $\dim (\text{Supp}(\mathcal{F})) \leq k$, then $f_*[\mathcal{F}]_ k = [f_*\mathcal{F}]_ k$. In particular, if $Z \subset X$ is a closed subscheme of dimension $\leq k$, then $f_*[Z]_ k = [f_*\mathcal{O}_ Z]_ k$. </p>
</article> 
</p>
<article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      See Chow Homology, Lemma <a href="/tag/02R6" data-tag="02R6">42.12.4</a>. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>
<p>
<article class="env-lemma" id="0B0N">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0B0N">Lemma <span data-tag="0B0N">43.6.2</span>.</a> Let $f : X \to Y$ and $g : Y \to Z$ be proper morphisms of varieties. Then $g_* \circ f_* = (g \circ f)_*$ as maps $Z_ k(X) \to Z_ k(Z)$. </p>
</article> 
</p>
<article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Special case of Chow Homology, Lemma <a href="/tag/02R5" data-tag="02R5">42.12.2</a>. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>
